= Decoy effect =

== Overview ==
The '''decoy effect''' or '''attraction effect''' occurs when adding an asymmetrically dominated option changes preferences between existing alternatives.

== Context dependence ==
If option C is clearly worse than option A on relevant dimensions but not similarly dominated by B, introducing C can increase selection of A relative to B.

== Significance ==
The effect challenges simple models in which preference between A and B is independent of irrelevant alternatives. Its magnitude depends on task design and choice representation.

== References ==
* Joel Huber, John W. Payne & Christopher Puto, “Adding Asymmetrically Dominated Alternatives”, ''Journal of Consumer Research'' 9 (1982): 90–98.
* context-dependent choice literature.

